    "problem_statement": "\n\n# TextFSM ClearAllVar bug with List values\n\n#### Description\n\nThe `OnClearAllVar` method in `TextFSMOptions` class is initializing the `_value` list with `[None]` instead of an empty list. This causes issues when working with List-type values, especially when using nested regex capture groups.\n\n#### Steps/Code to Reproduce\n\n```python\nimport textfsm\nimport io\n\n# Example 1: List values with nested regex capture groups\ntplt = 'Value List foo ((?P<name>\\\\w+):\\\\s+(?P<age>\\\\d+)\\\\s+(?P<state>\\\\w{2})\\\\s*)\\nValue name (\\\\w+)\\n\\nStart\\n  ^\\\\s*${foo}\\n  ^\\\\s*${name}\\n  ^\\\\s*$$ -> Record'\nt = textfsm.TextFSM(io.StringIO(tplt))\ndata = ' Bob: 32 NC\\n Alice: 27 NY\\n Jeff: 45 CA\\nJulia\\n\\n'\nresult = t.ParseText(data)\nprint(result)  # Incorrect result\n\n# Example 2: Simple List values\ntplt = 'Value List boo (on.)\\nValue hoo (tw.)\\n\\nStart\\n  ^$boo\\n  ^$hoo -> Next.Record\\n\\nEOF'\nt = textfsm.TextFSM(io.StringIO(tplt))\ndata = 'one\\ntwo\\non0\\ntw0'\nresult = t.ParseText(data)\nprint(result)  # Incorrect result\n```\n\n#### Expected Results\n\nFor Example 1, the result should be:\n```\n[[[{'name': 'Bob', 'age': '32', 'state': 'NC'}, {'name': 'Alice', 'age': '27', 'state': 'NY'}, {'name': 'Jeff', 'age': '45', 'state': 'CA'}], 'Julia']]\n```\n\nFor Example 2, the result should be:\n```\n[[['one'], 'two'], [['on0'], 'tw0']]\n```\n\n#### Actual Results\n\nThe results include `None` values in the lists, which breaks the expected behavior of List-type values in TextFSM templates.\n\nThe issue is in the `OnClearAllVar` method in `TextFSMOptions` class, which is initializing the `_value` list incorrectly."
  }
